"Kent Paul Dolan" <xanthian@well.com> wrote:

> ... [Good luck on defining "summons Kops".]

That one made me think for a moment.

Why bother?  Kops are just like any other non-self encounter.  By
their (hostile) actions, they will prompt the Fight Or Flight
decision.  No further definitions are necessary, except to program
the 'bot *not* to stand in a hallway and kick down a door below
dungeon level 1.

OTOH, as one of a list of possible strategies for gaining
experience and/or equipment once the 'bot is of sufficient
buffness, stealing something and then defeating the Kops might not
be a bad idea.  Again, no further definitions are necessary:  'bot
determines that more experience or more of certain types of
equipment have reached a higher priority than descending to lower
levels, 'bot examines surroundings and notices a shop, 'bot
decides to steal something.  The next set of actions simply
complete this mini-task.

Somewhat relatedly, the appearance of a "non-self that might
summon nasties" should trigger a "kill this thing immediately"
action.  Unless, of course, the 'bot has already placed a high
priority on gaining experience and/or equipment, and feels buff
enough to take on the nasties for the spoils of victory.

I guess I see such a 'bot as an ever-reprioritized collection of
priorities, a collection of strategies to meet them, and a
collection of risks associated with those strategies, perhaps more
like an expert system than one that has to learn everything on its
own.  For example, one priority is food.  Every turn, the 'bot
would assign an importance to that priority:  the higher the
hunger level, the lower the food supplies in inventory, the higher
the priority.  Eventually, the 'bot would decide that eating
something is more important and carries less risk than further
exploration and/or immediate attention to something else.  Then
the 'bot would select something to eat and then eat it (or even
pray once it got down to that point).

Given the obtuse nature in which we humans gain information from
Nethack, I believe that such a 'bot would have to be spoiled out
the wazoo.

